Developed a secure IoT-based smart locking system as a college project to improve the security and reliability of conventional locking mechanisms. The project integrates steganography, QR code authentication, Convolutional Neural Networks (CNN), encrypted image-based credentials, and microcontroller-based access control. The main objective was to provide a secure authentication mechanism where sensitive QR code information could be hidden within an image and used for authorized access.

The system was developed over a period of three years by a team of three members. ESP32/Arduino was used as the microcontroller platform, while Python and OpenCV were used for image processing, QR code handling, and security-related operations. A servo motor was integrated with the microcontroller to control the locking and unlocking mechanism. The system verifies the authentication credentials before allowing access, thereby reducing the risk of unauthorized entry.

My contributions included participating in the system design and development, implementing image steganography, working on QR code generation and authentication, integrating the ESP32/Arduino microcontroller, and controlling the servo motor for the locking mechanism. I also contributed to testing and troubleshooting the system to ensure reliable communication between the software and hardware components.

The project combines concepts from Internet of Things (IoT), Embedded Systems, Image Processing, and Cybersecurity. By combining hidden QR credentials with authentication and hardware-based access control, the system provides an additional layer of security compared with traditional locking systems. The project helped me gain practical experience in IoT development, Python programming, image processing, embedded systems, hardware integration, and secure authentication techniques. Overall, the project resulted in a functional smart locking prototype designed to provide secure, reliable, and technology-driven access control.



